\name{pmdplan_getting_started}
\alias{pmdplan_getting_started}
\title{Choosing PMD Designs and Evaluation Inputs}
\description{Workflows for planning actual data collection, applying PMD to existing datasets and evaluating fitted results, with explicit design names and input formats.}
\details{
For a ready-to-use empirical example, load \code{\link{affect}} with \code{data("affect", package = "pmdplan")}. It contains 9500 rows, 125 participants and 76 retained occasions per participant. Use \code{id = "N_index"}, \code{time = "time"}, and item columns \code{x1}--\code{x4} (positive affect) and \code{y1}--\code{y4} (negative affect). See its data help for item meanings, preprocessing, source and the separate CC BY-NC 4.0 data license.

Supply data with one row per participant and measurement occasion. Use items in pmd_forms and pmd_item in pmd_occasions to select the variables to which planned missingness is applied. Numeric, character, logical, and factor items are supported. Times may be numbers, dates, date-times, or occasion labels; ordered factors can specify the order of labels. Other columns are retained unchanged.

Use pmd_forms for item-level multiform designs. Matrix and anchor designs rotate forms across occasions; the anchor design retains a designated subset in every form. Random designs select forms independently with equal probability. If forms is omitted, subsets_per_form specifies how many subsets to combine. If subsets is omitted, supply factors (a named list mapping factors to items) and n_subsets to generate them. Set cfa_type="twolevel" to select anchors using within-level standardized loadings, or cfa_type="singlelevel" for single-level CFA. For design="anchor", supply anchors or specify cfa_type. Supplied anchors take priority and disable CFA. Generated subsets receive randomly selected items from each factor in counts differing by at most one; extra items go to subsets containing fewer items. In anchor designs, this rule applies to non-anchor items across the remaining subsets. Set \code{id} to the participant identifier column name and \code{time} to the measurement time column name, for example \code{id = "N_index"} and \code{time = "time"}. Participant identifiers may be numeric, character, or factor values. The time column may be numeric, character, factor, \code{Date}, or \code{POSIXct}. See \code{\link{pmd_forms}} for examples and the Chen and Liu (2026) reference.

Use pmd_occasions with design="balanced" to distribute participants evenly among measurement patterns, design="consistent" for the same pattern across participants, or design="truncated" to retain only the initial consecutive measurements. first_prop specifies the initial measurement proportion. For balanced and consistent designs, interval=1 measures every second Phase II occasion; interval=2 measures every third. Truncation uses only first_prop. See \code{\link{pmd_occasions}} for an example comparing designs with equal measurement counts.

To generate a measurement schedule without a dataset, supply \code{N}, \code{TT}, \code{items}, and either \code{item_design} or \code{wave_design} to \code{pmd_schedule}. Select the PMD items with \code{pmd_items}; other items remain scheduled at every occasion. For item-level designs (\code{"matrix"} or \code{"random"}), supply \code{item_design = list(design = "matrix", subsets = ..., subsets_per_form = ...)}, or specify \code{forms} in the list. Subsets are required, and shared items can serve as anchors without a separate design option. For wave-level designs (\code{"balanced"}, \code{"consistent"}, or \code{"truncated"}), supply a \code{wave_design} list containing \code{design}, \code{first_prop} and, for balanced or consistent designs, \code{interval}. Balanced designs divide participants as evenly as possible among measurement phases. Truncated designs retain only the first \code{floor(TT * first_prop)} occasions for the PMD items. The output has one row per participant and occasion, with columns named \code{id}, \code{time}, and the names in \code{items}. Each item column contains \code{TRUE} for scheduled measurement and \code{FALSE} for planned omission. Use \code{pmd_schedule(x)} to extract an existing schedule, optionally supplying \code{items} to include additional items. See \code{\link{pmd_schedule}} for complete examples.

Use pmd_batch for a list of existing replications and named PMD functions. mc.cores=1 is the default on all platforms. Windows uses parallel::parLapply with PSOCK workers; macOS/Linux use parallel::mclapply. Set a larger positive integer for multiple workers on any of these systems. The pmd_batch examples show matrix and balanced design functions. An output directory saves individual replication/design RDS files plus manifest.csv, without a duplicate combined RDS. Read each saved dataset with readRDS(path)$data. For text output, select the needed columns and use utils::write.table; see the pmd_batch example. Analyze the resulting data with \code{\link{analyze_mplus}} or in your chosen software. To generate complete replications first, use \code{\link{generate_mplus_data}}. It calls Mplus once with NREP set to replication, using a two-factor DSEM template with editable default items (Sy/Sx/Ty/Tx) and population_values, or custom modelstring sections. Set run=FALSE to inspect syntax before execution. It returns data.frames with id, time and items, usable as pmd_batch(data=generated$data, ...). Generation is separate from PMD analysis fitting and evaluation. See its help for the full parameter list, identification constraints and the complete assembled default input.

Use \code{\link{analyze_mplus}} for batch analysis. modelstring=NULL builds the complete default DSEM input from the template arguments. Custom modelstring is a complete input script, written unchanged with writeLines: no section merging, FILE lookup/substitution or automatic line wrapping. A character string/vector is one shared script; a list must have one complete script per dataset in manifest row order, including complete baselines and failures. For 500 replications and three PMD designs plus complete, supply 2000 list entries. R inputs are exported as data_repN_DESIGN.dat in each task folder; explicitly write that filename or the actual external DAT path in each custom script.

Use pmd_batch(include_complete=TRUE) to include unchanged inputs as complete comparison rows. mc.cores controls concurrent datasets on both platforms: Windows uses PSOCK/parLapply; macOS/Linux use mclapply. Custom scripts set their own PROCESSORS and iteration options. Use all fit$output paths and fit$metadata rows for evaluation, and specify max_iterations to match the actual Mplus cap. See analyze_mplus for complete examples and ordering/file-naming rules.

Evaluate results using evaluate_pmd(source="mplus", "mcmc" or "manual"). Mplus input is a vector of existing per-fit .out paths with metadata and a parameter map. First call \code{\link{obtain_map}} with path set to an output directory; it writes map.csv with a blank parameter column. Fill custom names only for parameters of interest and leave unwanted rows blank. Keep paramHeader, param and BetweenWithin (when present) unchanged. Read the edited CSV and pass it as map; unnamed rows are ignored. Use the chosen names in population_value. MCMC input is a list of mcmc.list objects with metadata. Manual input is a long table of per-replication point estimates, SDs, interval bounds and maximum PSR. Include every intended replication so failures remain in convergence-rate denominators. Supply n_factors or an explicit cutoff; all sources require PSR below cutoff. Mplus additionally checks its error-list flag and final iteration below max_iterations (default 50000; user configurable). The same model uses one cutoff for every replication. A single scenario can omit condition. population_value supplies parameter/value columns; manual max_psr belongs in estimates. MCMC point estimates are posterior medians. See evaluate_pmd for complete column schemas and examples.

evaluate_pmd automatically reports which designs meet the active criteria. Use screen_designs to rescreen selected parameters. Defaults are coverage in [0.91,0.98], power at least 0.80 for nonzero population values, and type I error in [0.025,0.075] for zero population values. Every cutoff is configurable. Relative-bias screening defaults to max_abs_relative_bias=0.10, requiring absolute relative bias no greater than 0.10; NULL disables this criterion. RMSE has no cutoff. ESS, valid rate, mean interval width and MCSEs are not computed.
}
\seealso{\code{\link{pmd_schedule}}, \code{\link{pmd_forms}}, \code{\link{pmd_occasions}}, \code{\link{pmd_batch}}, \code{\link{evaluate_pmd}}}
